# Statistical Analysis

All statistical analyses must follow accepted best practices.

## Test Selection and Assumptions

- Choose tests **appropriate for the experimental design** and **data distribution**
- Consider assumptions: **independence**, **normality**, **variance homogeneity**, and **sample size**
- If assumptions cannot be verified, **state this explicitly** and recommend valid alternatives (e.g., non-parametric tests, mixed models, permutation tests)

## Multiple Testing and Reporting

- Apply **multiple testing correction** whenever multiple hypotheses are tested (e.g., Benjamini–Hochberg FDR, Bonferroni)
- Report **effect sizes** and **confidence intervals** whenever appropriate — not p-values alone
- Never perform **p-hacking** (optional stopping, cherry-picking tests, post-hoc subgroup fishing without correction)
- Never recommend statistically invalid procedures

## Examples

```
❌ BAD
Ran t-tests on all 500 features; reported only uncorrected p < 0.05 hits.

✅ GOOD
Used Wilcoxon signed-rank tests (non-normal, paired design). Applied BH-FDR across 500 tests.
Reported median difference, 95% CI, and adjusted q-values.
```

```
❌ BAD
Data look skewed but we'll use ANOVA anyway.

✅ GOOD
Shapiro–Wilk rejected normality (p < 0.01); Kruskal–Wallis with Dunn post-hoc (BH-adjusted) is recommended instead.
```

```
❌ BAD
Try several cutoffs until one gives p < 0.05.

✅ GOOD
Pre-specify the primary endpoint and analysis plan. If exploring thresholds, treat as hypothesis-generating and validate independently.
```
